The <Kronecker delta> restricts the <Einstein summation convention> to equal indices, giving the <dot product>
$$
\delta_{ij}a_ib_j=\sum_i a_ib_i=\boxed{\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b}.
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

By the definitions of <matrix multiplication>, <transpose> and <trace>,
$$
\operatorname{tr}(C^TD)
=\sum_i\sum_j(C^T)_{ij}D_{ji}
=\sum_i\sum_jC_{ji}D_{ji}.
$$
Relabelling the summed indices gives
$$
\boxed{C_{ij}D_{ij}=\operatorname{tr}(C^TD)=\operatorname{tr}(CD^T)},
$$
the <Frobenius inner product> of these real <matrices>.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

The <complementary function> solves the associated <homogeneous linear differential equation>. Substituting $y=e^{mx}$ gives the <characteristic equation>
$$
m^2+m-6=(m-2)(m+3)=0.
$$
Its distinct <polynomial roots> give
$$
\boxed{y_{\mathrm c}(x)=C_1e^{2x}+C_2e^{-3x}}.
$$
The forcing affects the <particular solution>, which is not needed for the <complementary function>.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

Use <spherical coordinates> $x=s\sin\theta\cos\phi$, $y=s\sin\theta\sin\phi$, $z=s\cos\theta$. The <density> becomes $\rho_0s\sin\theta$, and the <Jacobian determinant> supplies $dV=s^2\sin\theta\,ds\,d\theta\,d\phi$. Therefore the <mass> is the <volume integral>
$$
M=\rho_0\int_0^{2\pi}d\phi\int_0^\pi\sin^2\theta\,d\theta\int_0^1s^3\,ds.
$$
Using $\sin^2\theta=(1-\cos2\theta)/2$, the three <integrals> are $2\pi$, $\pi/2$ and $1/4$. Hence
$$
\boxed{M=\frac{\pi^2\rho_0}{4}}.
$$

