= Solution
{parent=i}

Using the <complex conjugate>, $b\bar z+\bar bz=2\operatorname{Re}(\bar bz)$. The <locus> is therefore the <straight line>
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}(\bar bz)=-\frac c2}.
$$
In the <complex plane>, the quantity $\operatorname{Re}(\bar bz)/|b|$ is the signed <scalar projection> of $z$ in the direction $b$. Thus the <straight line> is perpendicular to $b$ and passes through $-cb/(2|b|^2)$. This description uses no separation of $z$ into real coordinates.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiplying the <complex numbers> gives $b\bar z+\bar bz=2(b_1x+b_2y)$. Hence the real <linear equation> is
$$
\boxed{2b_1x+2b_2y+c=0}.
$$

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Completing the square> directly with the <complex modulus> gives
$$
|z+b|^2=|b|^2-c.
$$
For $|b|^2>c$, this is the <circle> with <centre> $-b$ and <radius> $\sqrt{|b|^2-c}$. Equality gives just the point $-b$, and $|b|^2<c$ gives the empty <locus>. In particular, the nondegenerate answer is
$$
\boxed{\text{centre }-b,\qquad \text{radius }\sqrt{|b|^2-c}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Since $z\bar z=x^2+y^2$, the real <quadratic equation> is
$$
x^2+y^2+2b_1x+2b_2y+c=0.
$$
<Completing the square> makes its geometry explicit:
$$
\boxed{(x+b_1)^2+(y+b_2)^2=b_1^2+b_2^2-c}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A squared <complex modulus> is nonnegative. Conversely, every nonnegative right-hand side of $|z+b|^2=|b|^2-c$ can be attained. The necessary and sufficient condition is therefore
$$
\boxed{c\leq |b|^2=b_1^2+b_2^2}.
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

There is a single point exactly when the <radius> is zero:
$$
\boxed{c=|b|^2,\qquad z=-b}.
$$
Then $|z+b|^2=0$ forces this unique value of the <complex number> $z$.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Under the given <rotation>, $\bar z=\bar\alpha\bar w$ and $z\bar z=|\alpha|^2w\bar w=w\bar w$. Collecting terms gives
$$
a w\bar w+b\bar\alpha\bar w+\bar b\alpha w+c=0.
$$
Since $\overline{b\bar\alpha}=\bar b\alpha$, the transformed coefficients are
$$
\boxed{A=a,\qquad B=b\bar\alpha,\qquad C=c}.
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the real positive <dilation> factor, $\bar z=\alpha\bar w$ and $z\bar z=\alpha^2w\bar w$. Thus
$$
a\alpha^2w\bar w+\alpha b\bar w+\alpha\bar b w+c=0,
$$
and
$$
\boxed{A=a\alpha^2,\qquad B=\alpha b,\qquad C=c}.
$$
The original PDF specifies a dilation; the differently named transformation in the OCR is an error.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the <translation>, expand the products of <complex numbers> and collect the terms in $w\bar w$, $\bar w$, $w$ and the constant:
$$
a(w+\alpha)(\bar w+\bar\alpha)+b(\bar w+\bar\alpha)+\bar b(w+\alpha)+c=0.
$$
It follows that
$$
\boxed{A=a,\qquad B=a\alpha+b,\qquad C=a|\alpha|^2+b\bar\alpha+\bar b\alpha+c}.
$$
The last coefficient is real because $b\bar\alpha+\bar b\alpha=2\operatorname{Re}(\bar b\alpha)$.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For the reciprocal <Möbius transformation>, substitute $z=1/w$, $\bar z=1/\bar w$ and multiply by $w\bar w$, which is nonzero on the stipulated domain. This yields
$$
a+bw+\bar b\bar w+cw\bar w=0.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{A=c,\qquad B=\bar b,\qquad C=a},
\qquad
\boxed{A=0\iff c=0}.
$$
For a nondegenerate original <circle>, $c=0$ says that it passes through the origin, and its image is a <straight line>. The restriction $w\ne0$ must be retained: multiplying through does not authorize adding that excluded point. Degenerate choices can give a constant or an identically zero equation instead of a <straight line>.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $N=g+r$. Successive <conditional probabilities> account for the changing bag under <sampling without replacement>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(GGG)=\frac gN\frac{g-1}{N-1}\frac{g-2}{N-2}}.
$$
On this event no equality of the two colour counts has occurred, so the game's stopping rule does not interrupt these three draws.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

After one draw the two counts are either $(1,0)$ or $(0,1)$ and cannot agree. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\text{success after one draw})=0}.
$$
This is the elementary parity restriction behind the <first return time> interpretation of this game.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Success after two draws occurs on the two <mutually exclusive events> $GR$ and $RG$. With $N=g+r$, <sampling without replacement> gives
$$
\mathbb P(GR)+\mathbb P(RG)
=\frac gN\frac r{N-1}+\frac rN\frac g{N-1}
=\boxed{\frac{2gr}{N(N-1)}}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The equality must be a <first return time>: an order starting $GR$ or $RG$ would already have stopped at draw two. The only successful four-draw orders are therefore $GGRR$ and $RRGG$. Their <probabilities> under <sampling without replacement> add to
$$
\boxed{\frac gN\frac{g-1}{N-1}\frac r{N-2}\frac{r-1}{N-3}
+\frac rN\frac{r-1}{N-1}\frac g{N-2}\frac{g-1}{N-3}}
=\boxed{\frac{2g(g-1)r(r-1)}{N(N-1)(N-2)(N-3)}}.
$$
Here $N=g+r$. Counting all balanced four-draw orders would include games that have already stopped.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The two possible colour orders are $GGRR$ and $RRGG$. Thus the answer is
$$
\boxed{2}.
$$
They correspond to the positive and negative four-step excursions with <first return time> four.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Suppose the first draw is green. The excess of green over red must remain positive after draws one through five and return to zero at draw six. After deleting the first green and final red, the remaining four steps must form a <Dyck path>: they have equal colour counts and never go below zero relative to the new starting level. There are $C_2=2$ such orders, $GGRR$ and $GRGR$, giving $GGGRRR$ and $GGRGRR$ in the original game.

Interchanging green and red gives $RRRGGG$ and $RRGRGG$. Thus there are
$$
\boxed{4}
$$
orders. More generally, the <Catalan number> $C_{m-1}$ counts each sign of excursion with <first return time> $2m$.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $X_1$ and $X_2$ denote the first and second labels. <Sampling with replacement> restores the bag before the second draw, so conditioning on $X_1=x$ only specifies the threshold. For $1\leq x\leq g$, there are $x$ qualifying green balls and $x$ qualifying red balls. For $g<x\leq r$, every green ball and the first $x$ red balls qualify. Hence the requested <conditional probability> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X_2\leq x\mid X_1=x)=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{2x}{g+r},&1\leq x\leq g,\\[4pt]
\dfrac{g+x}{g+r},&g<x\leq r.
\end{cases}}
$$
The formulas agree at $x=g$ and give one at $x=r$. Labels outside the possible first-draw range do not define a conditioning event of positive <probability>.

= e
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Only a green ball carries label four. After it is removed, nine of the remaining nineteen balls are green. The <conditional probability> is
$$
\boxed{\frac9{19}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The specified colour tells us that one green ball has been removed. Thus <sampling without replacement> again leaves nine green balls among nineteen:
$$
\boxed{\frac9{19}}.
$$
The numerical label supplies no further change once this first colour is known.

= iii
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

There are two equally likely balls bearing label eight, one of each colour. The <conditional probability> that the removed ball was green is therefore $1/2$. By the <law of total probability>,
$$
\mathbb P(\text{second green}\mid X_1=8)
=\frac12\frac9{19}+\frac12\frac{10}{19}
=\boxed{\frac12}.
$$
Both possible first colours must be included because the observed label alone does not determine the colour.

= 13
