= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Differentiate the second <ordinary differential equation> and use the first to eliminate $x'$:
$$
y''=x'-\sin t=\beta y+\sin t-\sin t.
$$
Thus the reduced <second-order linear differential equation> is
$$
\boxed{y''-\beta y=0}.
$$
No solutions are lost: given a solution $y$, define $x=y'-\cos t$; then both original <ordinary differential equations> hold.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <characteristic equation> of the reduced <homogeneous linear differential equation> is $m^2-\beta=0$. Its <polynomial roots> change type with the sign of $\beta$, giving
$$
\boxed{y(t)=
\begin{cases}
C_1e^{\sqrt\beta\,t}+C_2e^{-\sqrt\beta\,t},&\beta>0,\\[3pt]
C_1+C_2t,&\beta=0,\\[3pt]
C_1\cos(\sqrt{-\beta}\,t)+C_2\sin(\sqrt{-\beta}\,t),&\beta<0.
\end{cases}}
$$
For $\beta=0$, the zero <polynomial root> is repeated, so the second independent solution is $t$. For $\beta<0$, the <polynomial roots> are the <complex conjugate> pair $\pm i\sqrt{-\beta}$, and the displayed <sine> and <cosine> give a real <basis> of solutions. The constants are arbitrary real numbers. In every case $x(t)=y'(t)-\cos t$ reconstructs the corresponding <general solution> of the coupled system.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <differential operator> on the left is $(D-3)^2$, where $D=d/dt$. Its repeated <characteristic root> suggests writing $y=e^{3t}v$. By the <product rule>,
$$
y'=e^{3t}(v'+3v),\qquad y''=e^{3t}(v''+6v'+9v),
$$
so the <ordinary differential equation> reduces to $v''=3(t+1)$. Two <antiderivatives> give
$$
v'=\frac32t^2+3t+A,\qquad
v=\frac12t^3+\frac32t^2+At+B.
$$
The <boundary conditions> imply $v(0)=1$ and $v(1)=0$, hence $B=1$ and $A=-3$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(t)=e^{3t}\left(\frac12t^3+\frac32t^2-3t+1\right)}.
$$
The calculation of $v''$ verifies the <ordinary differential equation>, and the bracket takes the values one and zero at the two endpoints. These <boundary conditions> determine both constants uniquely.

= 14
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

To distinguish the old and new coordinate descriptions, write $u(x,y)=U(\xi(x,y),\eta(x,y))$. Since $\xi_x=\eta_x=1$, the <multivariable chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{u_x=U_\xi+U_\eta}.
$$
Equivalently, the <differential operator> is $\partial_x=\partial_\xi+\partial_\eta$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The coordinate <partial derivatives> are $\xi_y=1/\sqrt y$ and $\eta_y=-1/\sqrt y$. The <multivariable chain rule> therefore gives
$$
u_y=\frac{U_\xi-U_\eta}{\sqrt y}.
$$
Since $\xi-\eta=4\sqrt y>0$, the expression entirely in the new coordinates is
$$
\boxed{u_y=\frac4{\xi-\eta}(U_\xi-U_\eta)}.
$$
The inverse coordinate formulas are $x=(\xi+\eta)/2$ and $y=(\xi-\eta)^2/16$, with domain $\xi>\eta$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Apply $\partial_x=\partial_\xi+\partial_\eta$ once more. The <chain rule> and <symmetry of second derivatives> give
$$
\boxed{u_{xx}=U_{\xi\xi}+2U_{\xi\eta}+U_{\eta\eta}}.
$$
Here and in the next part the second <partial derivatives> are understood with the usual <smoothness> required for this <partial differential equation>.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Differentiate $u_y=y^{-1/2}(U_\xi-U_\eta)$, retaining the <derivative> of the prefactor. The <product rule> and <multivariable chain rule> give
$$
u_{yy}=-\frac1{2y^{3/2}}(U_\xi-U_\eta)
+\frac1y(U_{\xi\xi}-2U_{\xi\eta}+U_{\eta\eta}).
$$
Using $y=(\xi-\eta)^2/16$, this becomes
$$
\boxed{u_{yy}=\frac{16}{(\xi-\eta)^2}(U_{\xi\xi}-2U_{\xi\eta}+U_{\eta\eta})
-\frac{32}{(\xi-\eta)^3}(U_\xi-U_\eta)}.
$$
The last term is essential because the coordinate change is nonlinear.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Substitute the <partial derivatives> obtained above. The first-order terms cancel exactly:
$$
\begin{aligned}
u_{xx}-\tfrac12u_y-yu_{yy}
&=U_{\xi\xi}+2U_{\xi\eta}+U_{\eta\eta}
-\frac{U_\xi-U_\eta}{2\sqrt y}\\
&\quad-\left(U_{\xi\xi}-2U_{\xi\eta}+U_{\eta\eta}
-\frac{U_\xi-U_\eta}{2\sqrt y}\right)\\
&=4U_{\xi\eta}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The transformed <partial differential equation> is therefore
$$
\boxed{U_{\xi\eta}=0,\qquad \xi>\eta}.
$$
In these <characteristic coordinates>, the mixed <partial derivative> is the only surviving term.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the given <Cartesian coordinates> and integrate with respect to $y$ first. Since $\cos y\,e^{\sin y}$ is the <derivative> of $e^{\sin y}$, the <double integral> becomes
$$
I=\int_0^\pi\cos x\left[ e^{\sin y}\right]_{y=0}^{y=x}\,dx
=\int_0^\pi\cos x(e^{\sin x}-1)\,dx.
$$
A direct <antiderivative> avoids any issue with the nonmonotonicity of $\sin x$ on this interval:
$$
\boxed{I=\left[e^{\sin x}-\sin x\right]_0^\pi=0}.
$$
The sign changes in $\cos x$ make this cancellation possible even though $e^{\sin y}$ is positive.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Here $y\geq0$, so the <absolute value> equals $y$. The <volume integral>, evaluated in <Cartesian coordinates>, is
$$
J=\int_0^1\int_0^x\int_0^{xy}xy\,dz\,dy\,dx.
$$
The integrand is constant with respect to $z$, giving
$$
J=\int_0^1\int_0^xx^2y^2\,dy\,dx
=\frac13\int_0^1x^5\,dx
=\boxed{\frac1{18}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Integrating first over $z$ multiplies by $e^y$. Split at $y=0$ to handle the <absolute value> correctly:
$$
J=\int_0^1x\left[\int_{-x/2}^0(-y)e^y\,dy+\int_0^xy e^y\,dy\right]dx.
$$
<Integration by parts> gives <antiderivatives> $(1-y)e^y$ and $(y-1)e^y$ for these two inner <integrands>. Their contributions are
$$
1-(1+x/2)e^{-x/2},\qquad (x-1)e^x+1,
$$
respectively. Thus
$$
J=\int_0^1\left[2x+(x^2-x)e^x-(x+x^2/2)e^{-x/2}\right]dx.
$$
For the remaining <integrals>,
$$
\int_0^1(x^2-x)e^x\,dx
=\left[e^x(x^2-3x+3)\right]_0^1=e-3,
$$
and
$$
\int_0^1(x+x^2/2)e^{-x/2}\,dx
=\left[-e^{-x/2}(x^2+6x+12)\right]_0^1
=12-19e^{-1/2}.
$$
Combining them yields
$$
\boxed{J=e-14+19e^{-1/2}}.
$$
The upper $z$ bound is $e^y$, as in the original PDF; an additional truncated version in the OCR must not be used.

= 16
