= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <parametric curve>, $\mathbf r'(t)=(2t,3t^2)^T$ and
$$
\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))=(t^6-2t^2,\ 2t^5-t^3)^T.
$$
The <line integral> is therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
&=\int_0^1\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))\cdot\mathbf r'(t)\,dt\\
&=\int_0^1(8t^7-3t^5-4t^3)\,dt\\
&=1-\frac12-1=\boxed{-\frac12}.
\end{aligned}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The relevant <mixed partial derivatives> agree:
$$
\partial_y(y^2-2x)=2y=\partial_x(2xy-y).
$$
The <vector field> is <smooth> on the entire <simply connected> <plane>, so this condition establishes that it is a <conservative vector field>. We can also construct its <vector-field potential> explicitly. From $\phi_x=y^2-2x$, take an <antiderivative> in $x$:
$$
\phi=xy^2-x^2+h(y).
$$
Then $\phi_y=2xy+h'(y)=2xy-y$ requires $h'(y)=-y$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\phi(x,y)=xy^2-x^2-\frac12y^2+C,\qquad \mathbf F=\nabla\phi}.
$$
The <fundamental theorem for line integrals> checks the previous answer: $\phi(1,1)-\phi(0,0)=-1/2$.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $\mathbf W=\nabla\times\mathbf F$ and $\mathbf v=\dot{\mathbf r}$. All <vector fields> and their spatial <partial derivatives> below are evaluated at $\mathbf r(t)$. The <product rule> for a <dot product> gives
$$
\frac d{dt}\bigl(\mathbf W(\mathbf r(t))\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}(t)\bigr)
=\frac{d\mathbf W(\mathbf r(t))}{dt}\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}(t)
+\mathbf W(\mathbf r(t))\cdot\ddot{\mathbf r}(t).
$$
Because the <vector field> has no explicit time dependence, the <multivariable chain rule> gives $d\mathbf W/dt=J_{\mathbf W}\dot{\mathbf r}$, where the <Jacobian matrix> has entries $(J_{\mathbf W})_{ij}=\partial_jW_i$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\left[(\nabla\times\mathbf F)\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}\right]
=\dot{\mathbf r}^{\,T}J_{\nabla\times\mathbf F}\dot{\mathbf r}
+(\nabla\times\mathbf F)\cdot\ddot{\mathbf r}}.
$$
The first term measures the change of the <curl> sampled as the point moves; the second measures the change in the <velocity vector> used in the <dot product>.

To give the result explicitly in $P,Q,R$, define the <directional derivative> operator $L=\dot x\partial_x+\dot y\partial_y+\dot z\partial_z$. Since
$$
\mathbf W=(R_y-Q_z,\ P_z-R_x,\ Q_x-P_y)^T,
$$
the same expression is
$$
\begin{aligned}
&\dot x\,L(R_y-Q_z)+\dot y\,L(P_z-R_x)+\dot z\,L(Q_x-P_y)\\
&\quad+\ddot x(R_y-Q_z)+\ddot y(P_z-R_x)+\ddot z(Q_x-P_y).
\end{aligned}
$$
For example $L(R_y-Q_z)=\dot x(R_{yx}-Q_{zx})+\dot y(R_{yy}-Q_{zy})+\dot z(R_{yz}-Q_{zz})$; the other two terms expand in the same way. Thus only the first and second spatial <partial derivatives> of $P,Q,R$, together with the <velocity> and <acceleration> of the <parametric curve>, occur.

= 17
{parent=Paper 2}
