= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <divergence> of the position <vector field> is $\nabla\cdot\mathbf r=3$. At height $z$, the horizontal cross-section is a <disk> of <area> $\pi(1-z^2)$. By the <divergence theorem>, the outward <flux integral> is three times the enclosed <volume>:
$$
\Phi(z_0)=3\pi\int_{-1}^{z_0}(1-z^2)\,dz
=3\pi\left[z-\frac{z^3}{3}\right]_{-1}^{z_0}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\Phi(z_0)=\pi(2+3z_0-z_0^3)=\pi(1+z_0)^2(2-z_0)}.
$$
A direct check separates the two pieces of the <surface integral>. On the unit <sphere>, the outward <unit normal> is $\mathbf r$, so $\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf n=1$ and the curved contribution is $2\pi(z_0+1)$. On the top <disk>, $\mathbf n=\mathbf e_z$, giving $\pi z_0(1-z_0^2)$. Their sum is the same <flux>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Differentiate the <flux>:
$$
\boxed{\Phi'(z_0)=3\pi(1-z_0^2)},\qquad
\Phi''(z_0)=-6\pi z_0.
$$
On $-1<z_0<1$, $\Phi$ is strictly increasing. Its limiting endpoint values are $\Phi(-1)=0$ and $\Phi(1)=4\pi$, and $\Phi(0)=2\pi$. It has horizontal limiting <tangents> at the endpoints, curves upward for $z_0<0$, curves downward for $z_0>0$, and has an <inflection point> at zero.

The <derivative> graph is a downward-opening <parabola>, approaching zero at both endpoints and attaining its maximum $3\pi$ at $z_0=0$.

\Image[paper-2-q17-flux.png]
{title=Flux through the truncated unit sphere and its derivative}
{description=Both curves are scaled by $\pi$. Endpoint circles show limiting values because the prescribed truncation parameter lies in $(-1,1)$. The <flux> increases from zero to $4\pi$, while its <derivative> is largest at the equator.}

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the two pieces of the <surface integral> directly; this avoids introducing an unnecessary second <derivative> of $f$. On the unit <sphere>, $\mathbf n=\mathbf r$ and $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n=f'(z)$. A spherical band between heights $z$ and $z+dz$ has <area> $2\pi\,dz$, so the curved <flux> is
$$
\Phi_{\mathrm{curved}}=2\pi\int_{-1}^{z_0}f'(z)\,dz
=2\pi\bigl(f(z_0)-f(-1)\bigr),
$$
by the <fundamental theorem of calculus>. On the horizontal top <disk>, $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n=z_0f'(z_0)$ and the <area> is $\pi(1-z_0^2)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\Phi(z_0)=2\pi\bigl(f(z_0)-f(-1)\bigr)
+\pi z_0(1-z_0^2)f'(z_0)}.
$$
As usual for these <surface integrals>, the first <derivative> is assumed sufficiently integrable for the displayed <fundamental theorem of calculus> evaluation; a continuous first <derivative> suffices. Mere pointwise <differentiability> without such an integrability assumption need not make an ordinary <flux integral> exist.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose
$$
\boxed{f(z)=z},
$$
or add any constant. Then $f'(z)=1$, so the <vector field> is exactly $\mathbf r$ and necessarily has the same <flux> as in part (a). The formula from part (b)(i) confirms this:
$$
2\pi(z_0+1)+\pi z_0(1-z_0^2)
=\pi(2+3z_0-z_0^3).
$$

