= Solution
{parent=a}

The <symmetric and antisymmetric parts of a matrix> are
$$
\boxed{S=\frac12(M+M^T),\qquad A=\frac12(M-M^T)}.
$$
Indeed $S+A=M$, while the <transpose> identities $(M^T)^T=M$ give $S^T=S$ and $A^T=-A$. Thus $S$ is a <symmetric matrix> and $A$ is an <antisymmetric matrix>. The decomposition is also unique: a <matrix> that is both symmetric and antisymmetric must equal its own negative and hence vanish.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take the <dot product> of the <orthogonal basis> expansion with $\mathbf u_j$. All terms except the $j$th vanish by <orthogonality>, leaving
$$
\mathbf u_j\cdot\mathbf x=\alpha_j\,\mathbf u_j\cdot\mathbf u_j.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\alpha_i=\frac{\mathbf u_i\cdot\mathbf x}{\mathbf u_i\cdot\mathbf u_i}}.
$$
The <basis> is not assumed normalized, so its squared lengths must remain in the denominators. Applying $S$ and using $S\mathbf u_i=\lambda_i\mathbf u_i$ gives
$$
S\mathbf x=\sum_i\lambda_i\alpha_i\mathbf u_i,
\qquad
\boxed{\beta_i=\lambda_i\frac{\mathbf u_i\cdot\mathbf x}{\mathbf u_i\cdot\mathbf u_i}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Induction on $p$ gives $S^p\mathbf u_i=\lambda_i^p\mathbf u_i$, including $p=0$. The absolutely convergent <matrix exponential> series may therefore be applied term by term to each <eigenvector>:
$$
C\mathbf u_i=\sum_{p=0}^\infty\frac{S^p\mathbf u_i}{p!}
=\left(\sum_{p=0}^\infty\frac{\lambda_i^p}{p!}\right)\mathbf u_i
=e^{\lambda_i}\mathbf u_i.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{eigenvectors }\mathbf u_i,\qquad \text{eigenvalues }e^{\lambda_i}}.
$$
For a repeated <eigenvalue>, every nonzero <vector> in its <eigenspace> remains an <eigenvector>. Because the real <exponential function> is <injective>, distinct real <eigenvalues> of $S$ do not merge under this transformation.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Since every <eigenvalue> is nonzero, $S$ is an <invertible matrix>. Multiplying $S\mathbf u_i=\lambda_i\mathbf u_i$ by the <inverse matrix> gives
$$
\boxed{S^{-1}\mathbf u_i=\lambda_i^{-1}\mathbf u_i}.
$$
Hence the <eigenvectors> are unchanged and the corresponding <eigenvalues> are $1/\lambda_i$.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Choose the unnormalized <eigenvectors>
$$
\mathbf u_+=(1,1)^T,\qquad S\mathbf u_+=3\mathbf u_+,
\qquad
\mathbf u_-=(1,-1)^T,\qquad S\mathbf u_-=-\mathbf u_-.
$$
Their <linear combination> giving the specified <vector> is
$$
\mathbf x=\frac12\mathbf u_++\frac12\mathbf u_-.
$$
Applying the reciprocal <eigenvalues> of the <inverse matrix> therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\mathbf y=\frac16\mathbf u_+-\frac12\mathbf u_-}.
$$
In <Cartesian coordinates>, this is $(-1/3,2/3)^T$, and direct <matrix multiplication> gives $S\mathbf y=(1,0)^T$.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The defining condition for an <antisymmetric matrix> forces
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}0&a\\-a&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
A(x,y)^T=(ay,-ax)^T.
$$
Its <dot product> with $(x,y)^T$ is zero, and its <Euclidean norm> is $|a|\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$. Thus it combines a right-angle <rotation> with a uniform <dilation> by $|a|$. More precisely, the <rotation> is clockwise for $a>0$ and anticlockwise for $a<0$. A direction-independent description is a clockwise right-angle <rotation> followed by multiplication by the signed scalar $a$.

A second application gives
$$
\boxed{A^2=-a^2I,\qquad A^2\mathbf v=-a^2\mathbf v}.
$$
Geometrically this is a half-turn combined with a <dilation> by $a^2$. Because $A$ is nonzero, $a\ne0$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $J=A/a$, so $J^2=-I$. The even and odd powers in the <matrix exponential> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
D
&=I\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^ka^{2k}}{(2k)!}
+J\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^ka^{2k+1}}{(2k+1)!}\\
&=I\cos a+J\sin a.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here the two scalar sums are the <Taylor series> for <cosine> and <sine>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{D=\begin{pmatrix}\cos a&\sin a\\-\sin a&\cos a\end{pmatrix},\qquad \theta=a\pmod{2\pi}}.
$$
With the usual convention that positive angles turn anticlockwise, this <rotation matrix> rotates by $-a$; the parameter in the form printed in the paper is $\theta=a$.

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
