= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <function> $f$ is <continuous> at $x_0$ if
$$
\boxed{\forall\varepsilon>0\ \exists\delta>0\ \forall x\in\mathbb R:
\quad |x-x_0|<\delta\ \Longrightarrow\ |f(x)-f(x_0)|<\varepsilon}.
$$
The choice of $\delta$ may depend on $\varepsilon$ and $x_0$, but must work for every $x$ in that neighbourhood. This is the <epsilon-delta definition of continuity>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <function> $f$ is <differentiable> at $x_0$ if there exists a finite real number $L$ such that
$$
\boxed{\forall\varepsilon>0\ \exists\delta>0\ \forall x\in\mathbb R:
\quad 0<|x-x_0|<\delta\ \Longrightarrow\
\left|\frac{f(x)-f(x_0)}{x-x_0}-L\right|<\varepsilon}.
$$
Then $f'(x_0)=L$. The puncture $0<|x-x_0|$ makes the <difference quotient> defined; requiring a finite real $L$ distinguishes an ordinary <derivative> from an infinite slope.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Along the first <sequence>, $\sin(1/x_n)=\sin(2n\pi+\pi/6)=1/2$, whereas along the second <sequence>, $\sin(1/x'_n)=\sin(2n\pi)=0$. The outer <sine> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{f_0(x_n)=\sin(\pi/2)=1,\qquad f_0(x'_n)=0}.
$$
Both <sequences> approach zero through positive values. For arbitrary $a$, the same calculation gives $f_a(x_n)=x_n^a$ and $f_a(x'_n)=0$.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $a>0$, the fact that <sine> is a <bounded function> gives $|f_a(x)-f_a(0)|\leq |x|^a$. Given $\varepsilon>0$, choose $\delta=\varepsilon^{1/a}$. If $|x|<\delta$, then $|f_a(x)-f_a(0)|<\varepsilon$. This proves <continuity> directly from its definition.

For $a\leq0$, use the first <sequence> from part (b), for which $f_a(x_n)=x_n^a$. When $a=0$ these values are one; when $a<0$ they tend to $+\infty$. In either case, for $\varepsilon=1/2$ every neighbourhood of zero contains a sufficiently late $x_n$ with $|f_a(x_n)-f_a(0)|\geq1/2$. This contradicts the definition of <continuity>. Hence
$$
\boxed{f_a\text{ is continuous at }0\iff a>0}.
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <difference quotient> at zero is
$$
\frac{f_a(h)-f_a(0)}h
=\frac{|h|^a}{h}\sin(\pi\sin(1/h)),\qquad h\ne0.
$$
For $a>1$, its <absolute value> is at most $|h|^{a-1}$. Given $\varepsilon>0$, set $\delta=\varepsilon^{1/(a-1)}$. Then $0<|h|<\delta$ makes the <difference quotient> have <absolute value> below $\varepsilon$. Thus $f_a'(0)=0$.

Conversely, along the second <sequence> the <difference quotient> is exactly zero. Any finite <derivative> would consequently have to be zero. Along the first positive <sequence>, however, the <difference quotient> equals $x_n^{a-1}$. It is one for $a=1$ and tends to $+\infty$ for $a<1$. In both cases it stays outside the $\varepsilon=1/2$ neighbourhood of zero for arbitrarily small positive increments. Thus no finite <derivative> exists. We obtain
$$
\boxed{f_a\text{ is differentiable at }0\iff a>1,\qquad f_a'(0)=0\text{ when }a>1}.
$$
In particular, $0<a\leq1$ gives <continuity> without <differentiability>.

= d
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For positive integers $n$, the summand is $a_n=\sin(\pi\sin(1/n))/n$. Since $0<\sin(1/n)<1$, these terms are positive. Apply the <limit comparison test> with the convergent <p-series> $\sum n^{-2}$:
$$
\frac{a_n}{n^{-2}}
=n\sin(\pi\sin(1/n))
=\frac{\sin(\pi\sin(1/n))}{\pi\sin(1/n)}\,
\pi\frac{\sin(1/n)}{1/n}
\longrightarrow\pi.
$$
Each sine ratio tends to one. The <limit comparison test> says that two positive-term <series> whose term ratio tends to a finite positive number are either both <convergent series>[convergent] or both <divergent series>[divergent]. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^\infty f_{-1}(n)\text{ converges absolutely}}.
$$
There is also a direct <comparison test for series>: $0\leq\sin(\pi\sin(1/n))\leq\pi\sin(1/n)\leq\pi/n$, giving $0\leq a_n\leq\pi/n^2$.

= 20
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $\Psi(\eta,\xi)=\psi(x,t)$. The inverse coordinate formulas $x=(\eta+\xi)/2$ and $t=(\eta-\xi)/(2c)$ are valid because $c>0$. The <multivariable chain rule> gives
$$
\partial_x=\partial_\eta+\partial_\xi,\qquad
\partial_t=c\partial_\eta-c\partial_\xi.
$$
Applying these operators twice and using the <symmetry of second derivatives>,
$$
\psi_{xx}=\Psi_{\eta\eta}+2\Psi_{\eta\xi}+\Psi_{\xi\xi},
\qquad
\frac1{c^2}\psi_{tt}=\Psi_{\eta\eta}-2\Psi_{\eta\xi}+\Psi_{\xi\xi}.
$$
Thus the <wave equation> is equivalent to
$$
\boxed{\Psi_{\eta\xi}=0}.
$$
For each fixed $\eta$, the <partial derivative> $\Psi_\eta$ has zero <derivative> with respect to $\xi$, so it is a <function> of $\eta$ alone. Taking an <antiderivative> in $\eta$ gives $\Psi=f(\eta)+g(\xi)$, where the integration constant may depend on $\xi$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\psi(x,t)=f(x+ct)+g(x-ct)}.
$$
Conversely, direct <partial differentiation> verifies the <wave equation> for every twice <differentiable> pair $f,g$. The two terms describe profiles moving left and right respectively at <wave speed> $c$ along the <characteristic lines for the one-dimensional wave equation>.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At $t=0$, the <initial conditions> imply
$$
f(x)+g(x)=u(x),\qquad c\bigl(f'(x)-g'(x)\bigr)=v(x).
$$
Differentiate the first relation and solve for the two <derivatives>:
$$
f'=\frac12u'+\frac1{2c}v,\qquad
g'=\frac12u'-\frac1{2c}v.
$$
Fix a reference point $x_*$ and set $V(x)=\int_{x_*}^xv(s)\,ds$. Taking <antiderivatives> gives
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac12u(x)+\frac1{2c}V(x)+K,\qquad
g(x)=\frac12u(x)-\frac1{2c}V(x)-K}.
$$
The constants must be opposite to preserve the first <initial condition>; the arbitrary <constant of integration> $K$ does not change $\psi$. Substitution yields <D'Alembert's formula>:
$$
\boxed{\psi(x,t)=\frac{u(x+ct)+u(x-ct)}2
+\frac1{2c}\int_{x-ct}^{x+ct}v(s)\,ds}.
$$
The <fundamental theorem of calculus> verifies that its time <derivative> at zero is $v(x)$, and its value at zero is $u(x)$. The oriented <integral> also makes the formula valid for negative $t$.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

An <antiderivative> of the initial <velocity> is $-2c e^{-s^2/2}$. Thus <D'Alembert's formula> gives
$$
\psi(x,t)=\frac1{2c}\left[-2c e^{-s^2/2}\right]_{x-ct}^{x+ct}
=\boxed{e^{-(x-ct)^2/2}-e^{-(x+ct)^2/2}}.
$$
This is a positive <Gaussian function> moving right and a negative <Gaussian function> moving left. Their cancellation at $t=0$ gives the zero initial displacement, while direct <differentiation> gives $\psi_t(x,0)=2cx e^{-x^2/2}$.

For every $t>0$, the profile is an <odd function> of $x$, vanishes at $x=0$, is positive for $x>0$ and negative for $x<0$, and tends to zero at both spatial infinities. The signs follow from $(x+ct)^2-(x-ct)^2=4xct$. The positive and negative lobes move farther apart as $t$ increases; for large separation they approach the individual unit-height <Gaussian functions> centred at $x=\pm ct$. At finite separation the actual extrema lie slightly beyond these centres: writing $a=ct$, the positive <global maximum> occurs at the unique $x>a$ satisfying $(x-a)/(x+a)=e^{-2ax}$.

\Image[paper-2-q20-waves.png]
{title=Oppositely signed Gaussian wave profiles at two positive times}
{description=Representative times with $ct_1=0.6$ and $ct_2=1.8$ show the <odd function> profile and increasing separation of its lobes. The individual component centres are $x=\pm ct$, while their superposition slightly shifts the actual extrema.}
